# Liquid Glass = real native, always

When the user mentions "liquid glass", "glass", "glassy", "frosted",
"blur" in a UI context, or asks to make something feel native on
iOS 26+: render **real** Liquid Glass via Apple's framework. Never
fake it in JS.

## ✅ Approved primitives (already installed in this repo)

- `@expo/ui/swift-ui` — `Host`, `Picker`, `ZStack`, `HStack`,
  `Capsule`, `RoundedRectangle`, `Button`, `Text`, `Namespace`,
  `GlassEffectContainer`.
- `@expo/ui/swift-ui/modifiers` — `pickerStyle('segmented')`,
  `tag(value)`, `glassEffect({ glass: { variant, tint, interactive },
  shape })`, `glassEffectId(id, namespace)`, `buttonStyle('glass' |
  'glassProminent')`, `animation(Animation.spring(...), value)` for
  sliding/morphing.
- `expo-glass-effect` — `GlassView` + `isGlassEffectAPIAvailable()`
  for nav-bar / overlay backgrounds.
- See `apps/mobile/src/components/native/IOSSegmentedControl.tsx`
  and `apps/mobile/src/components/native/Glass.tsx` for the
  reference patterns this project uses.

## ❌ Forbidden — never ship these as "glass"

- `backgroundColor: "rgba(...)"` + a `LinearGradient` overlay
  pretending to refract.
- React Native `BlurView` / `expo-blur` as a stand-in for Liquid
  Glass on iOS (it's iOS 7-era `UIVisualEffectView`, not iOS 26
  Liquid Glass).
- Any custom JS-drawn pill / track / thumb that *imitates* the
  glass material with shadows + translucent tints.
- Hand-composing a segmented control out of `Capsule` +
  `glassEffect` when the user wants the native iOS 26 look. On
  flat backgrounds the composition reads as a heavy gray track
  with an inset blob — visually nothing like Apple's Phone / Mail
  controls. Use `@expo/ui` `Picker` with
  `pickerStyle('segmented')` instead (see "Segmented controls"
  below). Historical note: `expo/expo#44739` claimed the SwiftUI
  Picker rendered the legacy bordered style on iOS 26 via
  `UIHostingController`; that turned out to be stale by 20 May
  2026 with `@expo/ui` 55.0.13 + iOS 26.4 — the real
  `UISegmentedControl` shows up with the correct Liquid Glass
  appearance on both simulator and device.

## Segmented controls — use the real `UISegmentedControl`

```tsx
import { Host, Picker, Text } from "@expo/ui/swift-ui";
import { pickerStyle, tag } from "@expo/ui/swift-ui/modifiers";

<Host matchContents>
  <Picker
    selection={value}
    onSelectionChange={(next) => onChange(next as V)}
    modifiers={[pickerStyle("segmented")]}
  >
    {options.map((opt) => (
      <Text key={opt.value} modifiers={[tag(opt.value)]}>
        {opt.label}
      </Text>
    ))}
  </Picker>
</Host>
```

SwiftUI's `Picker(.segmented)` lowers to a real `UISegmentedControl`
on iOS. Apple's iOS 26 SDK gives that control the Liquid Glass thumb
appearance automatically — no composition required.

## Required workflow when adding glass

1. Render the SwiftUI inside `<Host>` (real `UIHostingController`).
2. Apply `glassEffect({ glass: { variant: "regular", tint? } })`
   — never simulate.
3. Gate with `isGlassEffectAPIAvailable()` only for the
   *fallback path* on iOS < 26 / Android — always ship the real
   thing on iOS 26+.
4. If `@expo/ui` is missing a primitive you need, install it via
   `npx expo install` and run `npx expo prebuild --platform ios`
   + rebuild — do **not** invent a JS substitute.

If you catch yourself reaching for `rgba()`, `LinearGradient`,
`BlurView`, or a "fake glass" `View`, stop and use the SwiftUI
path instead.
